#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d075df6-453f-5ecc-ad12-d83d346032df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=8(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301480} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1e0cae-033a-58f1-bc21-33616af1195b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=8(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301481} {:answer/id #uuid "d96474cc-e09a-5429-9d2e-8594f2e6a3d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=4(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301482} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f2c6c2-2963-59fb-bb96-05c32b2ce4a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=8(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301483} {:answer/id #uuid "ba47b123-29fb-5295-86c2-cdd0ead646d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=8(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d075df6-453f-5ecc-ad12-d83d346032df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=8(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301480} :answer-field/id #uuid "89c55efa-1538-525f-bb1c-75d79c3dc25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25eb3505-f2cc-55ce-9408-26205e80e549" :question/math-academy-id "q-106500" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=4t,\\,y+5=2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+3}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{y+5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{y+5}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{y+5}{2} \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =8(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cadca9a-952d-5491-b921-3a5909d67b8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301487} {:answer/id #uuid "7953bd5c-fdb5-54b4-a64a-f33c99953715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7953bd5c-fdb5-54b4-a64a-f33c99953715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301488} :answer-field/id #uuid "3061b774-768d-5945-a930-8745bdd46602" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301486} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "460fda13-8ff8-575a-a3cf-2b8ef5ddcc18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "460fda13-8ff8-575a-a3cf-2b8ef5ddcc18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301490} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad12e0ad-90cf-5c2c-9566-a9e11b32e5ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41d94804-48b6-5be3-bce5-5a3693e0aa7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-323221" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=4{t}^{2},\\,y-1=8t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+2}{4},\\,t=\\frac{y-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-1}{8})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+2}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{64} & =\\frac{x+2}{4} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =64\\cdot (\\frac{x+2}{4}) \\\\ {(y-1)}^{2} & =16(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fcd0123-fbe8-574b-ab52-e1ad3647585d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fcd0123-fbe8-574b-ab52-e1ad3647585d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301493} :answer-field/id #uuid "49218b6d-88c5-533f-be7a-01f0a13802c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301492} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b9925a6-9f06-5ca1-b8d7-af5197d5cb66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b9925a6-9f06-5ca1-b8d7-af5197d5cb66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301495} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a2e027-ce5e-567b-ab57-514ff72fe68c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0253ece9-d579-587e-a8e2-8a9065f5b162" :question/math-academy-id "q-323230" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-4(x+1),\\,t=-(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-(y-2))}^{2} \\\\  & =(y-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4(x+1)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =-4(x+1) \\\\ {(y-2)}^{2} & =-4(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1e627b7-bfd6-534e-830d-90b90bf24d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301498} {:answer/id #uuid "6598d4bd-0f51-59f8-96b1-480f3601275c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301499} {:answer/id #uuid "809636ff-d992-572c-a491-dbcaab56b899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301500} {:answer/id #uuid "36b5d164-c361-542e-8465-94bac038821d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301501} {:answer/id #uuid "96e52685-a506-5142-9b93-9931257df6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y+2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6598d4bd-0f51-59f8-96b1-480f3601275c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301499} :answer-field/id #uuid "6baca86a-7538-54f8-b930-9937030a4c0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84bc7a7c-9e6f-5cc0-a103-68903527aa4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-84840" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,2),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,6),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b76ff9a-20ce-58dd-bb5b-a2c91fe9986e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301505} {:answer/id #uuid "af9df707-eabb-52a0-bce2-cc41d484d781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,y+2=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301506} {:answer/id #uuid "c88ca086-1050-5d05-ac5b-1586a1b40966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301507} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a45b32-3143-507e-adbd-6eae182996ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=-{t}^{2},\\,y+2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301508} {:answer/id #uuid "fa501ae9-403a-52fc-b10a-a5abbc6d0417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4={t}^{2},\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c88ca086-1050-5d05-ac5b-1586a1b40966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301507} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc2f9049-4ae2-592c-b08f-435e027d0fcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23c1a911-6224-553e-84b2-80970be0d8e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-84843" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/ba3d2c8719c97911d2339548f08601b07bcfe555d59dbb058a157836c45a34fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-4,2),$ and passes through the point $(-5,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-4,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+4=(-1){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(-1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t.\n$$"}]}
